<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Confederation of Cologne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1367">1367</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> was an alliance formed in Cologne in November 1367 by Hanseatic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> towns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark">Valdemar IV of Denmark</a>, joined by Holstein, Mecklenburg and Swedish nobles. Its fleets ravaged the Danish and Norwegian coasts and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> in 1368. The war ended with the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> of 1370, the high point of Hanseatic power in the Baltic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/confederation-of-cologne.jpg" alt="Confederation of Cologne" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: various · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Die_Gartenlaube_(1860)_b_821.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark">Valdemar IV of Denmark</a> had spent much of his reign reducing the autonomy of his nobles, enlarging the crown lands and putting his finances in order. He then reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1360 and took Gotland in 1361 after a decisive victory at the Battle of Visby. Because Visby and Gotland lay within the Hanseatic sphere, the League declared war, but after it nearly lost at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingborg">Helsingborg</a> it agreed a truce with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> at Vordingborg in 1365.</p><p>In 1367 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hansetag">Hansetag</a> met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, chosen for its nearness to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> towns, which were vital to the war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, Rostock, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wismar">Wismar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Kulm</a> and Thorn were present, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampen">Kampen</a>, Harderwyk, Elburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and Briel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The members agreed to levy taxes on all goods in their ports and, to simplify collection, chose the currencies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> for the main regions of the League. Each member was to provide a fixed number of ships and men, and departure dates were set for towns across the Baltic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> so that the combined fleet could assemble in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Oresund</a> for the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. Unusually for a medieval treaty, the text was written not in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> but in Middle Low German, the language of the Hanseatic towns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Support was uneven. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, exhausted by earlier wars, gave money but no military help, and inland towns, untouched by the war at sea, refused to aid it; the Westphalian towns gave nothing. The Confederation nonetheless won allies in Mecklenburg, Holstein and among Danish nobles opposed to Valdemar, broke the truce of Vordingborg and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. The years after the peace marked the golden age of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>.</p><p>The Confederation outlived the war by fifteen years. When the League gave up the Danish forts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> in 1385, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> wished to keep it, but the other towns chose to dissolve it. In the 15th century several groups tried to form similar alliances, known as Tohopesaten, without success.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Confederation of Cologne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1367 the towns formed the <b>Confederation of Cologne</b> with Holstein, Mecklenburg and Swedish allies, ravaged the Danish coasts and took Copenhagen, and the Peace of Stralsund of 1370 gave them control of the Scanian fortresses for fifteen years and a voice in the Danish succession.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Military</a></li></ul></section>
